// @run
// @log 7
// @log 7
// @log 7
// @hint redundant type annotation: `holder` is inferred as `{fn: ({[string]: i32}) => i32}`
// The `H` annotation the hint names is LOAD-BEARING: it is what routes the field's arrow
// through `annParamKind`, which is the second of the two producers this file pins. Declared
// rather than deleted, exactly as `closure-value-nullable-niche-param.vl` declares its nine.
// A function VALUE that takes a MAP parameter — D701, the arm `cloParamTok` was missing and
// the arm `annParamKind` was missing beside it.
//
// The floor was `emitClosureValueCore`'s `cloSigKeyExt(fe) == ""`: with no `$fnsig` token for
// a map param, the whole key stayed un-interned and the value was refused as "a function
// value may not take a nullable or map parameter (or return one)". That is the compiler's own
// capability voice, and the DIRECT SPELLING is what showed it was a gap and not a rule —
// `function lamc(x: {[string]: i32}) { return x }` runs today, and a map RESULT
// (`const mk = (n: i32) => m`) ran too, so only the param position was unwired.
//
// WHAT MADE IT LOOK UNWIRABLE was the slot, not the position: the comment that stood at the
// exclusion read `repSigTokOfKind("map")` as a RESULT token and feared "a slot-less `m`".
// `repSigTokHasSlot("map")` has been true since the token was minted, so `m<shape>;` is what
// every decoder already parses, and `paramStructIdxOf` → `annValtypeSlotOf` has carried the
// `map` → `mapAnnShape` pairing all along — the digit IS the heap slot the callee's own
// functype declares.
//
// THREE POSITIONS, because the two `$fnsig` producers have to agree on all of them:
//   * `lamc` — the AST producer alone (a lambda bound to a const, called directly);
//   * `apply` — BOTH producers, since the annotated arrow parameter keys through
//     `annParamKind` while the passed lambda keys through `cloParamTok`. Before the second
//     arm this shape said "function-value call arity has no interned signature" — the two
//     producers disagreeing, not a mis-lowering;
//   * `holder.fn` — the same disagreement through a struct FIELD's arrow annotation.
type H = { fn: ({[string]: i32}) => i32 }

function apply(f: ({[string]: i32}) => i32, m: {[string]: i32}) { return f(m) }

const c: {[string]: i32} = Map()
c["k"] = 7

const lamc = (x: {[string]: i32}) => x["k"] ?? 0
print(lamc(c))

print(apply(lamc, c))

const holder: H = { fn: lamc }
print(holder.fn(c))
